<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="act-temp">
  <title>Equivalence Class Examples</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Find the equivalence classes of the following relations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the set of all the ways you can write the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4 in a square.  Define the relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> as follows.  For each pair of squares <m>s_1,s_2 \in A</m>, we say that <m>s_1Rs_2</m> if <m>s_1</m> can be rotated so that it looks exactly like <m>s_2</m>.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write down two squares that are related to one another.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write down two squares that are <em>not</em> related to one another.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find all equivalence classes of <m>R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A = \{ 0,1,2,3,4,5 \}</m>, and define <m>E</m> on <m>A</m> by <m>aEb</m> precisely when <m>a^2 \equiv b^2</m> (mod 6).  Do parts (a) through (d) above for <m>E</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>